WEBVTT

00:00.350 --> 00:05.169
Rail engineering teams work across standards, maintenance instructions, change
notices and asset records.

00:05.169 --> 00:13.323
Before a change is accepted or work is authorised, the reviewer needs the
governing source, the correct revision and a defensible route

00:13.323 --> 00:14.806
back to the evidence.

00:14.806 --> 00:21.478
When those relationships are difficult to inspect, scarce engineering time is
spent reconstructing the document trail instead of

00:21.478 --> 00:22.590
judging the work.

00:23.590 --> 00:30.135
A single asset class can depend on infrastructure standards, rolling-stock
instructions, maintenance procedures, technical bulletins

00:30.135 --> 00:31.881
and local change records.

00:31.881 --> 00:34.063
One document may supersede another.

00:34.063 --> 00:36.681
A referenced clause may have moved.

00:36.681 --> 00:39.735
Terminology and effectivity can differ between revisions.

00:39.735 --> 00:42.354
The challenge is not simply search.

00:42.354 --> 00:47.590
It is establishing which source governed the question at the relevant time.

00:48.590 --> 00:51.801
Choose one asset class and one review workflow.

00:51.801 --> 00:57.421
Agree the authoritative documents, representative historic revisions and the
questions the evaluation must answer.

00:57.421 --> 01:02.239
Set the access boundary, the reviewers and the evidence standard in advance.

01:02.239 --> 01:09.464
Cortessa is then tested against matched tasks from the current process,
including difficult documents, missing references and questions

01:09.464 --> 01:11.070
that should be refused.

01:12.070 --> 01:18.682
Cortessa is intended to operate on a dedicated sovereign workstation inside an
agreed organisational boundary.

01:18.682 --> 01:24.853
The approved corpus, local index, on-device model and source viewer remain
within that environment.

01:24.853 --> 01:29.261
Runtime operation does not depend on a public cloud service.

01:29.261 --> 01:34.550
The rail organisation defines permitted users, documents, identity controls
and security posture.

01:35.550 --> 01:38.474
Audit is the centre of the Cortessa workflow.

01:38.474 --> 01:44.323
It works across the bounded engineering corpus rather than waiting for one
question at a time.

01:44.323 --> 01:52.365
It can surface a reference the corpus does not hold, terminology that changes
between documents, an instruction that appears to rely on

01:52.365 --> 01:54.924
a superseded source, or uncertainty about effectivity.

01:54.924 --> 01:59.310
Each candidate is presented for investigation, not treated as an automatic
finding.

02:00.310 --> 02:05.137
Every audit result must lead back to the passage that created it.

02:05.137 --> 02:11.172
The reviewer can open the source, inspect surrounding wording and confirm the
revision and context.

02:11.172 --> 02:16.401
If the evidence is absent, Cortessa can refuse rather than fill the gap.

02:16.401 --> 02:24.045
The engineer decides whether the discrepancy is real, irrelevant or already
controlled, then records the outcome through the existing

02:24.045 --> 02:24.447
process.

02:24.447 --> 02:28.470
Verified shows provenance; it does not establish the engineering conclusion.

02:29.470 --> 02:36.723
Asset context, engineering risk, operational constraints and applicable
standards cannot be delegated to a language model.

02:36.723 --> 02:41.710
Cortessa can make the evidence route and uncertainty easier to inspect.

02:41.710 --> 02:48.963
The authorised engineer determines applicability, resolves apparent conflicts
and decides whether a change or instruction is

02:48.963 --> 02:49.417
acceptable.

02:49.417 --> 02:55.310
Cortessa does not approve engineering change, establish ROGS compliance or
control railway operations.

02:56.310 --> 02:58.418
Reviewers need more than fluent output.

02:58.418 --> 03:04.390
They need the source route, the document state and the policy governing what
the system may present.

03:04.390 --> 03:08.606
Identity, document currency, assertion policy and egress posture remain
visible governance concerns.

03:08.606 --> 03:15.280
Local activity can be recorded in a ledger so the organisation can inspect
what the system did and when.

03:15.280 --> 03:20.550
That record can support an evidence package; it does not replace the
engineering approval record.

03:21.550 --> 03:26.799
Compare Cortessa with the current workflow using the same documents and tasks.

03:26.799 --> 03:34.235
Measure time to the governing source, reviewer effort, accepted and rejected
findings, false-finding burden, unanswered questions and

03:34.235 --> 03:34.673
traceability.

03:34.673 --> 03:38.172
Include stale references, conflicting wording and absent sources.

03:38.172 --> 03:40.796
Record corpus preparation and setup effort.

03:40.796 --> 03:48.670
The result should show where Cortessa helps, where it fails and what must
change before any wider use.

03:49.670 --> 03:56.517
Bring one bounded rail engineering workflow, an agreed document set and the
people authorised to judge it.

03:56.517 --> 03:59.336
Evaluate the evidence route before considering expansion.

03:59.336 --> 04:01.350
Start a conversation with Cortessa.
